(project pricklypear-workstreams
 (doc "The orchestration layer: turns the triage board into a managed delivery
  pipeline where each workstream is a list, each card has a status pipeline +
  comment/event thread + acceptance criteria, and the wall-clock work (spec,
  implement, review) is executed by LOCAL agents (laptop pi ralph/subagent loops)
  claiming cards from the habitat. The habitat is the record + queue; the laptop
  is the executor. NO agents run inside the prod image (jobs runner stays for
  small eval jobs). Supersedes the cron-generated static workstreams mirror note
  (2026-09-25: retired — a mirror that reports nothing is worse than no mirror).")
 (status planned)

 (* agent note (|2026-09-26 PLAN PASS (sessions as runs + runners + stream
    pipelines, roerick discussion): the executor story upgrades from
    fire-and-forget subprocesses to OBSERVABLE RUNS — with the session IS
    the run record (roerick, same pass — a separate run datum was drafted
    then rejected: no data.u.<user>.run.* rows ever exist). (1) SESSIONS AS
    RUNS: a glochid session carries identity, timestamp, model, history,
    resumability; card linkage is a header key, status is a terminal marker
    line, reclaim is FEED INACTIVITY on the card, and the card feed is the
    habitat-side projection in BOTH residences (glochid posts through the
    bridge; the native agent posts directly). (2) RUNNERS: glochid is the
    TARGET executor residence (sessions-persist + the loop on_msg sink give
    run status and progress comments for free); pi -p and the autolith
    adapter become fallback PP_FLOW_PI runners, not the direction of
    travel. (3) STREAM PIPELINES: UI-affecting vs under-the-hood work split
    into per-stream pipelines — pure list-columns data per the
    pipeline-model resolution order, only the triage placement rule is new.
    Spec review basis: local-first.borg/lens/replication review 2026-09-26
    — glochid has NO lens write path today (glochid/lib surface.ml: the
    lens-backed row is the deferred unification), so glochid posts card
    comments THROUGH THE BRIDGE (/api/eval); transcripts stay local
    (.agent/sessions/), never tree rows (2026-09-08 write-cost incident).|) *)
   (* agent note (|OBITUARY / SUPERSESSION (2026-10-16; lands in the SAME
      commit that registers borge/run-primitive.borg - gregor review point 1,
      binding: never ratified later): the 09-26 stance above - 'the session
      IS the run record'; 'no data.u.<user>.run.* rows ever exist' - is
      SUPERSEDED FOR SIDECAR RUNS. Durable run rows DO exist now, in the
      SHARED space data.s.run.* (the run-primitive chapter): the unattended
      drain needs state that is durable, resumable, and visible from any
      device (cheap lease, expensive state) - more than a laptop-local
      session JSONL can give. THE SESSION REMAINS the transcript record
      (local JSONL, never tree rows); the card feed REMAINS the human-facing
      record; the USER-scoped data.u.<user>.run.* ban itself STANDS. RATIFIED
      by gregor's review 2026-10-16 (D4 Q1/Q2).|) *)

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; RELATIONSHIPS (what it is / isn't)
 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 (section scope
  (doc "Extends borge/work-triage.borg (board + triage-pass + issue-board bridge).
   Adds: PER-WORKSTREAM lists (Basecamp-project shaped; one list = one
   workstream, sharing the same column taxonomy), CARD DETAIL v2 (comments
   thread + transition events interleaved, acceptance criteria, target branch),
   per-column AGENT RECIPES (the instruction template for the work each column
   represents), the CLAIM/BRIEF protocol (how a local pi executor claims a card,
   gets a self-contained brief, works, and writes back a transition + comment),
   SESSIONS AS RUNS (a glochid session IS the run record: card linkage in
   the header, a terminal status marker, the card feed as the habitat-side
   projection), PROGRESS COMMENTS (the agent posts milestone comments
   mid-run; transitions stay executor-owned), per-stream
   PIPELINES (UI-affecting vs under-the-hood cards on different column sets),
   the LIVE /workstreams face, and standup v2. Does NOT build an in-image agent
   runtime, does NOT re-spec core todo data model, does NOT touch auth/delegation
   beyond parameter-passing (owner creds or a delegated token).")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp (card detail v2 + recipes)" "libs/standup/lib.pp (v2)"
              "libs/workstreams/lib.pp (face + claim/probe helpers, planned)"
              "scripts/pp-flow.sh (local executor, planned)")
  (status planned))

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; THE DATA MODEL (all Nopales, no kernel changes)
 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 (section shared-store
  (doc "DECIDED 2026-10-01 (roerick): the todo store is a SHARED OPEN
   SPACE. The board, its lists, and its cards are collaboration furniture —
   humans and their agent identities work the SAME board, see each other's
   cards, and read each other's comments. Per-user gardens are the WRONG
   home and their persistence is the root of the drift this chapter
   inherited (two boards, gregor's 147 cards invisible to admin, admin's
   83 invisible to gregor, and an agent that cannot see the human's
   backlog).

   MECHANISM (substrate already shipped, no new kernel type):
   `data.s.<space>.<type>.<id>` from branching.shared-data-spaces, with
   OPEN spaces (migration 030) giving every authenticated identity implicit
   membership (the 'anonymous' sentinel explicitly excluded — guests keep
   per-user data). The chat lobby is the precedent; todos are the second
   consumer.

   ADMITTED TYPES (type-level routing, v1 per the spaces spec):
     todo-list, todo-item, todo-comment, todo-event
   These four are admitted to ONE open space (name `todo`, owner =
   whoever seeds first). Admission routes FUTURE writes; it is a
   type-level home, not a per-row flag.

   CONSEQUENCE — EVERY todo IS NOW SHARED BY DEFAULT. That is intended:
   this is a team board, not a personal to-do app. Personal/intimate
   checklists had no home before and still have none; if that need
   appears, it is a NEW type (or row-granular sharing, the spaces v2
   deferral), never a silent per-user fallback.

   STRANDED ROWS ARE MIGRATED, NOT ABANDONED. Pre-admission rows keep
   their `data.u.<user>.*` paths forever (admission is future-writes
   only), so the declared 'stranded rows stay' stance of spaces S1 is
   OVERRIDDEN HERE for these four types: the adoption migration moves
   every `data.u.*.<todo-type>.*` node into the space, preserving id,
   body, author, and created_at. A user's private pre-adoption backlog is
   thereby PUBLISHED TO THE TEAM — deliberate, recorded, and the reason
   the migration ships behind an explicit one-time verb rather than
   silently at boot.

   NOTE-TODO IDENTITY: `todo-list`/`todo-item` are the CARD datum. A
   note's outline node with a non-empty `todo` field is a DIFFERENT
   storage shape (kind='heading' under data.note.u.<user>.*, shared by NO
   one). Unifying the two is section note-todos, and it is the org-mode
   promise this chapter must keep.")
  (implements "migrations/029_data_spaces.sql" "migrations/030_open_spaces.sql"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp (space adoption + ensure)")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "todo-list/todo-item/todo-comment/todo-event are admitted to the open `todo` space"
         "two distinct identities each see every card and every list the other created"
         "a non-member ('anonymous') cannot read the shared board"
         "the adoption migration moves pre-existing data.u.* todo rows into the space with id/body/author/created_at preserved"
         "after adoption no todo datum remains under data.u.<user>.*")))

 (section workstream-lists
  (doc "A workstream is a todo-list with title = stream name (glochid, saguaro,
   town, …) and the shared column taxonomy via its `columns` field (set with
   kanban/columns-set; todo/state/columns-for-list already reads it). The
   shared `Board` list is the cross-stream overview. New intake lands in the
   workstream's TODO column; the triage pass or the owner places it.

   'SHARED' IS NOW LITERAL (section shared-store): the lists and their
   cards live in the open `todo` space, so 'shared' is a property of the
   STORE, not of which garden a reader happens to be in. Cross-garden
   identity is dropped as a concept here — there is one board. The named
   list ids in this chapter (Board, glochid, the AUTO lane) therefore
   refer to rows in the space and resolve for every identity.")
  (status implemented)  ;; lists exist; relocated to the space by shared-store
  (verify
   (test "each workstream list carries the same column set as Board"
         "every open board card sits in a named column"
         "a workstream list created by one identity is visible to another identity")))

 (section shared-register
  (doc "The issues register is a BOX surface, not the operator's garden. The Board list moves into a shared space so every authenticated user on the box can read the register and file cards into it, while pipeline/column transitions stay operator/worker-side. Single object per issue (the 09-28 convergence) is preserved — the card IS the issue; no ledger twin comes back.
   STORAGE/SEMANTICS (kw rules):
   - the Board list datum (0b21ffaa, and the 'Board' title resolution) moves to a SHARED space outside data.u.* and outside branch-chain inheritance — a card created by a guest must NOT pin a child-branch copy (that fork-local divergence is exactly the 2026-10-02 vanish);
   - todo/list/all merges garden lists + shared lists, so notes/issues/board-list-id finds 'Board' for EVERY identity — the (todo/list/ensure-inbox) fallback is retired for the register path;
   - writes: any AUTHENTICATED identity may create cards in the incoming lane (TODO); reads: open to all authenticated users on the box; anonymous stays denied (register content is box-internal);
   - column/transition control stays operator/worker-side — guests file and follow, they do not triage (LAW 2 symmetry: the queue's one-way doors stay with the operator);
   - every file ATTEMPT (ok or denied/failed) rows the activity ledger (product-experience.borg activity-ledger) — the 2026-10-02 case where an agent's report may have been attempted and silently vanished is the repro: a report is a knock, and knocks must never be silent;
   - migration moves the existing Board into the shared space, list + card ids preserved.")
  (implements "migrations/034_shared_board.sql"
              "image/lib/tree_store.ml"
              "image/lib/crud.ml"
              "image/lib/sync.ml"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp (todo/item/add-space, todo/list/all merge, todo/list/all-shared)"
              "libs/notes/lib.pp (notes/issues/board-list-id pair, notes/issues/add shared write)"
              "image/test/shared_register_test.ml")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|2026-10-02 marcus codex test: with the register garden-scoped, a guest filing an issue writes their own garden Inbox — operator queue never sees it; gregor's 09-02 card documented the same semantic ("writes always persisted to the caller's garden"). Empirics from the test: marcus was asked to have his agent file; zero marcus-authored datums exist store-wide; the attempt is untraceable with today's observability. Filed as 2026-10-02 DESIGN card; spec'd here instead of patching lib.pp in place.|) *)
  (* agent note (|2026-10-03 evidence correction (additive; semantics untouched): the note above was written BEFORE the agent's filing window — the codex agent DID file; all 9 issues landed 17:25-19:17 UTC in marcus's own garden Inbox (data.u.bWFyY3Vz0.dG9kby1pdGVt0.*), invisible to the operator register because every todo pool is caller-prefix-scoped (todo/tree/rows -> node-path(caller)). All 9 imported to the Board 2026-10-03 with provenance fields; one was eaten by a deferred-mirror (type,id) collision during the cross-garden move (Board card 8887656c, recovered by recreate). Reinforces the section: operator register blindness across gardens is the actual outage; the shared-space fix is the durable answer.|) *)
  (verify
   (test "a non-operator authenticated identity sees the Board in (todo/list/all) and (notes/issues/add …) lands ONE card ON that list — no garden fallback, single object per issue"
         "every authenticated user on the box can read /issues; anonymous is denied"
         "pipeline/column transitions of guest-filed cards remain operator/worker-side (guests create; they don't triage)"
         "a denied or failed file attempt appears as an attributed ledger row — no silent vanish"
         "existing list + card ids survive the move to the shared space")))

 (section stream-pipelines
  (doc "Per-stream pipelines for UI-affecting vs under-the-hood vs
   fully-mechanical work (2026-09-26 plan pass; AUTO lane added 2026-09-27
   after the 0fac2ef3 dogfood run showed a pure kernel bug funneled through
   UI-REVIEW and parked on the human gate). ALREADY SUPPORTED by the
   pipeline-model resolution order (the list's columns, else the global
   default — a pipeline is data, so the split is writing lists, not code.
   THREE streams:

   FACES — pipeline TODO → CODE → UI-REVIEW → HUMAN-REVIEW → DONE. Anything
   touching routes, rendering, or interaction. The UI-REVIEW recipe demands
   screenshot + a11y evidence per the agent UI harness
   (docs/agent-ui-harness.md). The human gate stays: rendered output is the
   product and only roerick signs off on it.

   KERNEL — pipeline TODO → CODE → LOGIC-REVIEW → HUMAN-REVIEW → DONE.
   Non-UI but high-blast-radius work: schema migrations, auth, store,
   promote-adjacent kernel changes. The LOGIC-REVIEW recipe demands dune
   build + runtest + borge drift evidence. The human gate stays because the
   failure modes are invisible until prod.

   AUTO — pipeline TODO → CODE → LOGIC-REVIEW → DONE. NO HUMAN-REVIEW
   column: a LOGIC-REVIEW PASS writes DESTINATION: DONE and pp-flow lands
   it there; the card closes on agent evidence alone. Qualifies when ALL
   hold: kind ∈ {BUG, CLEANUP, SMELL, docs}; no UI surface (no route,
   render, or interaction change); acceptance is machine-checkable (an
   existing test, smoke script, or eval expression can witness it); no
   schema migration; no prod promote (pp-flow briefs already forbid
   promote — the promote script remains the human gate for deploys, which
   bounds AUTO blast radius to the repo + garden branches). Safety story:
   the reviewer is structurally independent (each column transition is a
   separate pp-flow run, so the LOGIC-REVIEW reviewer is a fresh session,
   never the implementer grading itself), and the CODE recipe's mandatory
   build/test/drift evidence gates advancement. FAIL-LOOP CAP: if the card
   thread (todo-events + comments) already shows two LOGIC-REVIEW FAILs for
   the same defect, the reviewer escalates with DESTINATION: HUMAN-REVIEW
   instead of looping CODE forever.

   The payoff is PER-STREAM RECIPES (todo/recipe-set per list): FACES gets
   the UI-REVIEW harness recipe, KERNEL/AUTO get the kernel evidence recipe,
   and AUTO's LOGIC-REVIEW recipe overrides the default PASS → HUMAN-REVIEW
   with PASS → DONE. The remaining NEW bit is PLACEMENT: the triage pass
   (or intake) needs the rule for which stream a new card lands in — a
   triage-pass addition, not a pipeline change (see work-triage
   triage-pass).")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp (columns + recipes — both exist; per-stream config is data)"
              "work-triage triage pass (placement rule)")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "a faces-stream card, a kernel-stream card, and an auto-stream card resolve DIFFERENT effective pipelines from their lists' columns fields"
         "the triage pass places a new intake card in the stream its kind calls for"
         "an AUTO LOGIC-REVIEW PASS moves the card to DONE, not HUMAN-REVIEW (DESTINATION honored by pp-flow)"
         "the AUTO list's stored columns contain no HUMAN-REVIEW column"
         "a LOGIC-REVIEW PASS on an AUTO card with an empty DESTINATION advances to DONE, never HUMAN-REVIEW")))

 (section default-pipeline
  (doc "The GLOBAL DEFAULT pipeline is the ENGINEERING taxonomy, not the
   legacy checklist. borge/workstreams.borg pipeline-model resolves an
   item's effective pipeline as own override > list columns > global
   default; the global default is `todo/state/list`, which was still the
   original 5-state checklist ("" TODO DONE WAITING CANCELLED).
   CONSEQUENCE (audited live 2026-10-01): every list WITHOUT a `columns`
   field — all of Launch, PP Development, Secondary, the 13 unused AUTO
   lists, gregor's 36 ws-flow-smoke lists — resolves to a 5-state machine
   that shares only TODO/DONE with the engineering vocabulary. A card
   moved to SPEC, PARKED, or HUMAN-REVIEW on such a list renders OUTSIDE
   its own pipeline (the trailing 'other' bucket), and a cycle can jump
   into WAITING, a state no workstream ever uses.

   DECISION: the global default becomes the canonical engineering
   sequence, ordered so a bare `todo/cycle` is a SAFE, MEANINGFUL
   advance:

     todo/state/list = ("" TODO SPEC CODE LOGIC-REVIEW UI-REVIEW
                         HUMAN-REVIEW DONE PARKED)

   RATIONALE: this is the same ordered-list-of-state-names the pipeline
   model already treats as data (no kernel type, no enum — a pipeline is
   a list of strings). Making the DEFAULT equal the work-triage taxonomy
   means a list that never configures `columns` still behaves sanely:
   every work-triage state is a first-class position rather than an
   'other bucket' orphan, and no list can drive a card into a state the
   board cannot render.

   THE LEGACY CHECKLIST IS NOT DELETED, it is DERIVED: WAITING and
   CANCELLED remain reachable states for lists that want them, expressed
   as that list's own `columns` value (pipeline-model resolution (b)). A
   personal checklist list sets columns explicitly; the DEFAULT is
   engineering. `todo/state/list` stays a Nopales list (data), so
   re-deciding this is editing one list, never a migration.

   REJECTED ALTERNATIVE: keep the 5-state default and make every list
   declare columns. Rejected because the default is the value a NEW list
   gets by omission — the safe default must be the one that cannot strand
   a card outside its own pipeline, and requiring discipline at list-creation
   time is exactly the drift that produced 13 empty AUTO lists and 36
   smoke lists with no columns at all.

   CYCLE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: TODO -> SPEC -> CODE -> LOGIC-REVIEW ->
   UI-REVIEW -> HUMAN-REVIEW -> DONE -> PARKED -> TODO. `todo/cycle` on
   a fresh card therefore advances TODO -> SPEC (the honest next step for
   unspecified work), not TODO -> DONE. Lists that want a different
   forward motion set `columns`.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/state/list"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/state/todo-next-in (cycle order)")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "todo/state/list equals TODO SPEC CODE LOGIC-REVIEW UI-REVIEW HUMAN-REVIEW DONE PARKED"
         "a card on a columns-less list can be set to every work-triage state and stays in that list's pipeline (no other-bucket)"
         "todo/cycle on a TODO card of a columns-less list lands on SPEC"
         "a list with explicit legacy columns (WAITING/CANCELLED) still cycles only its own set")))

 (section note-todos
  (doc "THE ORG-MODE PROMISE, and the piece this chapter inherited broken.
   Inspiration is org-mode: a TODO is a MARK ON A NODE, not a separate
   object you go somewhere else to create. You type a keyword on a heading
   in the document you are already writing, and the item shows up in the
   agenda. Everything else — the board, the pipeline, the executor — is a
   view over that.

   THE FAILURE THIS CORRECTS. `unified-todos` (notes.borg) shipped only
   a ONE-WAY SCRAPE: `todo/items-html`'s 'from-notes' view walks
   `notes/all-todos` and renders note nodes read-only-ish. Three defects
   made that promise hollow, all verified live 2026-10-01:
     (1) ONE-WAY. A node's todo state and a card's state are separate
         storage with no link; a board card moved to CODE does not move
         the note node, and vice versa. The user sees a note item that
         'isn't the same thing' as the board item.
     (2) PER-USER AND INVISIBLE. Note nodes live at
         `data.note.u.<user>.<slug>.h.<nid>` — a per-user path, NOT a
         datum, and NOT admitted to any space. A note todo in one
         identity's garden is therefore invisible to every other identity
         and to every executor agent. Since the board is now shared
         (section shared-store), a note todo was the ONE work item that
         could not be worked by a team.
     (3) NO LINKAGE FIELD. Nothing on a todo-item recorded which note node
         produced it (no source_ref), so even a deliberate copy could not
         be reconciled, reopened, or closed-out once made. This is why the
         old spec parked it as 'no dual-copy until promote (later)' — it
         was RIGHT to refuse the unspecified copy, and the missing piece
         was the link, not the copy.

   THE CONTRACT (what 'make a node a todo' now means):

   1. MARK IN PLACE IS FREE AND PRIVATE. Setting a node's `todo` field
      (the existing `outline/set-todo` / `notes/cycle-node-todo` path)
      stays exactly what it is: a one-field write on a heading node, no
      card, no space, no round trip to the board. Personal notes keep
      working offline and unmigrated. Marking a node does NOT publish it.

   2. PROMOTION CREATES THE SHARED WORK ITEM. A new verb —
      `todo/promote-node` (slug, nid [, list-id]) — takes a note node and
      creates a `todo-item` in the shared `todo` space, carrying an
      explicit LINK back to its origin. The card's fields derive from the
      node: title from the node title, notes from the node body, `state`
      seeded from the node's todo keyword through the pipeline speaker
      (a node keyword SPEC maps to the SPEC column). The link is two
      fields on the card body:
        origin_kind = "note"
        origin_ref  = "<slug>#<nid>"
      `origin_ref` is the reconciliation key. It is what makes the copy
      SAFE where the old spec rightly refused one: the pair can always be
      found, compared, and re-joined.

   3. IDENTITY IS BIDIRECTIONAL AND STATE IS AUTHORITATIVE IN ONE PLACE.
      The CARD owns the pipeline state (section pipeline-model — the card
      machine is canonical). The NODE mirrors it for display: when a card
      with an `origin_ref` transitions, the node's `todo` field is written
      to the same state in the SAME transaction (the same single-owner
      discipline as issue cards, notes/issues/card-sync-ledger). A node
      whose keyword changes while it has a linked card updates the CARD
      (promotion is idempotent on origin_ref — re-promoting returns the
      existing card, never a duplicate).

   4. THE BOARD SHOWS IT ONCE. `/todos` and `/kanban` render the CARD, not
      a second scraped copy of the node: the 'from-notes' view becomes a
      filter over cards whose `origin_kind = "note"`, with a back-link to
      the note. The old scattergun `notes/all-todos` walk stays only as the
      source for UNPROMOTED nodes in the 'notes (unpromoted)' section, so
      a user can see what is in their notes and not yet on the board.

   5. SHARING FOLLOWS THE CARD, NOT THE NOTE. A promoted card is shared
      because the card is (section shared-store). The NOTE remains in the
      owner's garden: an agent working the card sees the node body through
      the card's `notes` (copied at promotion, refreshed on node-body
      change), and can write back a completion comment without ever being
      granted read access to the private note tree. This is the deliberate
      privacy boundary: publish the WORK ITEM, never the notebook page.

   WHAT THIS IS NOT: not a claim that note nodes are work items. An
   UNPROMOTED node is not on the board and has no pipeline position. The
   pipeline-model sentence 'a note-todo item is any tree node whose
   effective pipeline resolves' describes the converged end state; the
   tractable v1 is this PROMOTION BRIDGE, which delivers the org-mode feel
   (mark in the note, work on the board) without moving the note tree into
   a shared compartment or inventing a second status machine for nodes.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/promote-node + origin_ref reconciliation"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/item/set-state (origin write-back)"
              "libs/notes/lib.pp (unpromoted section + back-links)"
              "libs/kanban/lib.pp ('from-notes' as a card filter)")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "setting a node's todo keyword writes only that node (no card, no space row) and does not publish it"
         "todo/promote-node creates ONE shared card whose origin_ref is <slug>#<nid>, and re-promoting returns that same card"
         "a card transition writes the linked node's todo field in the same transaction"
         "changing a linked node's keyword updates the card's state"
         "a second identity sees the promoted card on the board and can move it without read access to the note"
         "an unpromoted node appears in the notes (unpromoted) section and NOWHERE on the board")))

 (section card-comments
  (doc "Two new datum types, seeded by the todo lib (deftype on load):
   `todo-comment` (fields: item_id, body; author + ts come from the row
   metadata / version history) and `todo-event` (fields: item_id, from_state,
   to_state; auto-written by every state transition via todo/state/next + the
   cycle/move writers). The card detail page interleaves events and comments
   chronologically (Linear-style activity feed). Comments are the ONLY place
   cross-agent context is recorded; transitions are the ONLY signal for
   standup and claim logic.
   STORAGE (2026-09-25): like todo-item itself these datums are USER-scoped in
   the one-tree — node-path resolves data.todo-comment to
   data.u.<user>.todo-comment, and the RLS floor confines reads/writes to the
   caller's user garden. The thread therefore lives under the writing ACCOUNT;
   the claim/brief protocol runs executors under owner creds or a single
   shared executor account (auth/delegation is parameter-passing only, per
   scope), so everyone on a card sees the same feed. All reads/writes go
   through node-path (todo/comment/* — todo/event/* — todo/feed) and
   auto-scope; never hardcode a shared data.<type> path.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp deftype todo-comment todo-event"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp card detail v2")
  (status implemented)  ;; orchestration tasks 2+3 (2026-09-25: comments/events/feed, transition events)
  (verify
   (test "a comment on a card appears on its detail page thread"
         "a state transition appends a todo-event row"
         "feed interleaves events+comments in time order")))

 (section acceptance
  (doc "Each card carries optional `acceptance` (criteria body) and `branch`
   (target branch for the work). These ride the todo-item datum like
   notes/assignee; the brief renders them. Enables 'any agent can pick up a
   card cold' because the card names the deliverable, the target, and the
   done-test.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp")
  (status implemented))  ;; orchestration task 4 (2026-09-25: acceptance + branch fields)

 (section branch-domain
  (doc "A card's `branch` field names a branch in ONE OF TWO branch systems,
   and a bare branch name is ambiguous:
     * GIT (branch_kind=\"git\") — a git branch in the laptop repo (master,
       a feature branch). Repo-side work: .borg spec, OCaml, scripts, tests.
       The executor commits and pushes there; remote is `wyo`.
     * HABITAT (branch_kind=\"habitat\") — a Nopales/Postgres branch ON the
       habitat (system/main, <user>/personal, <user>/dev). The executor
       define/route/promotes there; it NEVER exists in the git repo and a
       git-commit to it is meaningless.
   These are different substrates with different source-of-truth rules
   (branching: kernel/spec -> git; product Nopales -> PG branches). The
   INCIDENT that motivated this section (2026-09-27): a repo-side card was
   tagged `admin/dev` — a HABITAT branch — and the executor committed to git
   `master`. That was the correct action, but only because the executor knew
   the card was repo-side; nothing in the card or the brief said so. A card
   is therefore NOT complete until its branch domain is explicit.
   RULES:
   1. `branch` + `branch_kind` ride the todo-item datum; `branch_kind` is
      \"git\" | \"habitat\" | \"\" (unset). Set both with
      `todo/item/set-target` (or set-kind then set-branch).
   2. An UNSET domain is AMBIGUOUS, never a default. The brief renders it as
      an explicit `[DOMAIN UNSET]` warning plus a standing instruction to
      STOP AND ASK rather than guess; it does not silently pick a substrate.
   3. The brief always renders the domain (`branch: master [git]`) and a
      BRANCH DOMAIN explainer, so an executor picking the card up cold knows
      which substrate the branch lives in.
   4. A card whose work is unambiguously repo-side may leave `branch` empty
      (\"work on the repo default\"); an empty branch is not an error. What is
      forbidden is an unset domain on a non-empty branch.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/item/branch-kind todo/item/set-branch-kind todo/item/set-target"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/flow-brief (BRANCH DOMAIN block)"
              "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/detail-html + todo/ui/detail (domain badge)")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "a card with branch but no branch_kind renders [DOMAIN UNSET] + the ask-instead-of-guess instruction in its brief"
         "a card with branch_kind=git renders branch + [git] + 'commit to this branch in the laptop repo'"
         "a card with branch_kind=habitat renders [habitat] + 'do NOT git-commit to it'"
         "a card with no branch renders '(none)' + 'work on the repo default'"
         "todo/item/set-target writes branch and branch_kind in one call")))

 (section column-recipes
  (doc "The workstream list datum carries a `recipes` field: a dict mapping
   COLUMN NAME -> instruction template (text). E.g. CODE: 'you are the
   implementer for Pricklypear; branch <branch>; implement <title>; acceptance: <acceptance>;
   build with dune; live smoke on <target>; when done move the card to
   LOGIC-REVIEW and comment what you shipped + how reviewed.' A recipe is the
   bridge between the record and the executor — it IS the oracle step boiled
   down to instructions. The brief fn renders card + column recipe + context
   (repo path, branch, host, creds ref) into one self-contained prompt.
   IMPLEMENTED (orchestration task 6, ship 2026-09-27): functions live in the
   todo family as todo/recipes-for-list (the list's EXPLICIT recipes dict as
   an assoc list), todo/recipe (the EFFECTIVE text for one column: explicit
   non-empty recipe else default-family else generic fallback), and
   todo/recipe-set (write one recipe; empty text removes that column's key).
   NAMING NOTE: the build plan originally named these kanban/recipes-for-list
   + kanban/recipe-set + kanban/recipe, but libs/kanban is off-limits to the
   orchestration loop (Do-NOT rule) and master's convention keeps one clean
   namespace per family file — these operate on the todo-list datum, so they
   live in the todo family. The built-in default family (todo/recipes-default)
   covers the shared work-triage taxonomy SPEC / CODE / LOGIC-REVIEW /
   UI-REVIEW / HUMAN-REVIEW / DONE / PARKED / TODO as self-contained
   instruction texts (they reference 'the card above' — the brief prepends
   the card's title/acceptance/branch/state), so every list gets actionable
   instructions before anyone configures a per-list override.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp (todo/recipes-*)" "libs/todo/lib.pp flow/brief")
  (status implemented))  ;; orchestration task 6 (2026-09-27: todo/recipes-* + default family)

 (section session-is-the-run
  (doc "The RUN RECORD IS THE GLOCHID SESSION — no run datum type. A glochid
   session (glochid.borg sessions-persist: append-only JSONL, header
   {t:session, id, ts, model}, one msg line per provider message, resumable,
   /sessions surface) already carries identity, timestamp, model, full
   history, and resumability — everything a run record was supposed to buy
   over a scraped pp-flow log. What a session needs to SERVE as the run
   record: (1) CARD LINKAGE — one key in the session header (card: the
   item-id); (2) STATUS — one terminal marker line ({t:done, status:
   succeeded / failed / parked, summary}) appended by the loop driver;
   'running' is no terminal marker + a file still being written;
   (3) RECLAIM — the claim lease references the session
   (glochid:<session-id>@<ts>) and staleness is FEED INACTIVITY (claimed +
   no comment/event in N minutes), evaluable from any device — pid liveness
   was the laptop-local hack this retires, and a terminal marker in the
   session is a definitive 'run over' a pid can never give.
   THE CARD FEED IS THE HABITAT-SIDE RUN RECORD IN BOTH RESIDENCES: glochid
   posts progress comments through the bridge (the memory-bridge pattern,
   /api/eval under the executor's identity); the native in-image agent
   (agent-native.borg) posts them directly. The transcript stays wherever
   the loop ran (laptop .agent/sessions/ or the native store) — never tree
     rows. No data.u.<user>.run.* rows ever exist: no tree-write churn on
     prod, nothing to prune, no sync story to reason about (comments already
     replicate). SCOPE AMENDED (2026-10-16): the blanket no-run-datum
     conclusion no longer holds for SIDEcar runs - borge/run-primitive.borg
     (registered in the same commit) stores durable run rows in the SHARED
     space data.s.run.*, never under data.u.*; the transcript + card-feed
     division of labour survives unchanged: comments are the human-readable
   progress NARRATIVE, todo-events are column transitions, the session is
   the machine-readable RUN — and 'session' means the same thing on the
   laptop and in the image.")
  (implements "glochid/lib/sessions.ml (session header card key + terminal marker, planned)"
              "scripts/pp-flow.sh (lease references session id, planned)")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "a run's session header carries its card id and the session tail carries a terminal status marker"
         "the claim lease is glochid:<session-id>@<ts> and reclaim triggers on feed inactivity, not a pid probe"
          "under this model no run datum type exists (superseded 2026-10-16 for SIDEcar runs: shared-space data.s.run.* rows per borge/run-primitive.borg); the card feed is the habitat-side record")))

 (section claim-brief-protocol
  (doc "THE local executor contract (laptop pi, NOT the image):
   1. CLAIM: the executor (or roerick) sets `assignee` on the card (=claim;
      existing todo/item/assign machinery — landing gregor's assignment work
      provides this on master).
   2. BRIEF: `(todo/flow-brief card-id)` renders the full prompt; for a ralph
      loop the brief IS the plan file; for a subagent it's the task. NAMING
      NOTE: the build plan named this flow/brief; a `libs/flow` package would
      be a NEW family file, and the orchestration loop's Do-NOT rule limits
      it to todo/standup/workstreams libs — so the brief lives in the todo
      family as todo/flow-brief (implemented orchestration task 7).
   3. WORK: the agent reads the brief, does the work on the named branch.
   4. WRITE-BACK: the agent moves the card to the next column
      (todo/state/next-for-list through todo/cycle) and posts a todo-comment
      describing what it did + evidence. A transition to a REVIEW column is
      the 'ready for roerick' signal; the human gate is a real column
      (HUMAN-REVIEW), never an agent.)
   The claim lease is a pid/ts on the assignee field to prevent double-spawn;
   a stale lease (>12h) is reclaimable.
   SESSION-LEASE AMENDMENT (2026-09-26 plan pass): the pid/ts assignee
   lease becomes a SESSION lease (glochid:<session-id>@<ts>) once runs move
   to glochid (session-is-the-run) — a session reference is meaningful to
   any device with the lens, staleness is claimed + no feed activity in N
   minutes, and the session's terminal marker is the definitive 'run over'
   the pid probe can never give. PROGRESS COMMENTS: the agent posts
   milestone todo-comments MID-RUN (recipe instruction: 'post progress
   comments as you hit milestones via todo/comment/add — but never move the
   card yourself; the executor script owns transitions'). Comments are safe
   to open up to the agent because they never touch state (the double-write
   bug class is transitions-only); the write-back script keeps exclusive
   ownership of todo/cycle / todo/set-state. In the glochid residence this
   is the loop's on_msg sink (sessions-persist) hooked to the bridge —
   turn-boundary summaries post themselves; pi/autolith cannot do this
   cleanly, which is a reason runs move to glochid (see glochid-runner).
   UNATTENDED DRAIN (2026-09-27, the autonomy half): every run above needs a
   human to NAME a card, so 'let gregor/the habitat run it' still meant a
   person in the loop. `pp-flow.sh --drain [list-id] [N]` removes that: it
   picks its own work — queue order (priority, then position, then id, via
   todo/item/queue), filtered to ACTIONABLE columns (--states, default
   TODO,CODE), unleased cards only (assignee empty), minus excluded kinds
   (--exclude-kinds, e.g. BUG). Each card executes as a SEPARATE
   `--run` subprocess on purpose: that preserves the AUTO lane's safety
   property (each column transition is an independent run, so a LOGIC-REVIEW
   reviewer is never the session that implemented the change) — collapsing
   the loop into one process would destroy it. A failed card STOPS the drain
   by default (PP_FLOW_DRAIN_CONTINUE=1 to press on), because consecutive
   failures usually share a root cause (a provider outage, a broken build)
   and pressing on just burns leases; the exit code is the failure count so a
   scheduler can alert. The loop is still a SCHEDULER around pp-flow, not an
   in-image agent runtime (single-image: no agents in prod) — cron/gregor
   invokes it; the habitat stays the record + queue.
   DESTINATION VALIDATION (2026-09-27): an agent-named DESTINATION is
   AGENT-AUTHORED TEXT and is now validated against the card's actual column
   set (todo/state/columns-for-list for the card's own list) before
   write_back honors it; an unknown or unverifiable column falls back to the
   safe one-column advance and says so on stderr. This matters most on the
   AUTO lane, where a wrong DESTINATION either resurrects the human gate or
   writes a state the list does not define.
   writes a state the list does not define.")
  (implements "scripts/pp-flow.sh" "libs/todo/lib.pp todo/flow-brief")
  (status implemented)  ;; orchestration tasks 7+8 (2026-09-27: todo/flow-brief + scripts/pp-flow.sh)
  (verify
   (test "executor claim → brief contains title+acceptance+column recipe"
         "write-back lands an event + comment and advances exactly one column"
         "--drain picks a claimable TODO card with no human named card and advances it one column"
         "--drain skips leased cards, non-actionable columns, and excluded kinds"
         "an agent DESTINATION outside the card's column set is refused and the one-column advance is used"
         "a failed card stops the drain unless PP_FLOW_DRAIN_CONTINUE=1"
         "stale claims are reclaimable")))

  (section autolith-runner
   (doc "The claim/brief local executor is pluggable via PP_FLOW_PI (default
     `pi -p`). The AUTOLITH RUNNER (scripts/pp-flow-autolith.sh) is a
     PP_FLOW_PI adapter that routes one brief to Autolith's headless run-job
     instead of an interactive pi session, so a pp-flow card can be worked by
     a detached Lisp agent on the laptop.
     CONTRACT (pp-flow.sh calls PP_FLOW_PI as `$PI_RUNNER \"<brief text>\"`):
     on success print the job's terminal summary to stdout as the transcript
     (pp-flow scrapes the EVIDENCE / DESTINATION lines from it) and exit 0
     iff the job SUCCEEDED; a run-job crash, a non-SUCCEEDED status, or an
     unextractable :RESULT exits non-zero so pp-flow releases the claim and
     does not advance the card.
     JOB ENVELOPE ((:autolith-job :version 1 :id <str> :role \"executor\"
     :prompt <brief> :input nil :output-contract (:type :string)
     :timeout-seconds <n>)) is the autolith native task contract v1; the
     executor's :RESULT is the string that becomes the transcript.
     MODEL PINNING (lambda-symbolics/autolith#76): the executor role's
     :models (\"@auto\") is validated statically, so the real model must be a
     custom id registered in ~/.config/autolith/init.lisp and pinned per
     invocation via AUTOLITH_MODEL, not a builtin-only name.
     ENV: AUTOLITH_MODEL (init.lisp-registered model id; the adapter defaults
     it to `syn:large:text`, the Synthetic provider, for unattended/gregor
     runs — an explicit env value still wins), AUTOLITH_BIN (default
     `autolith`), PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_PERM (default `sandbox`; `full` only for
     cards that must touch the live repo), PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_TIMEOUT (default
     3000s; raised from 1800 after a real card hit that wall).
     EFFORT PREFLIGHT (2026-09-27): the role file pins :reasoning-effort
     :medium and run-job validates the ROLE's effort against the pinned model
     BEFORE any provider call; the role value beats
     AUTOLITH_REASONING_EFFORT (verified live). A model that rejects medium
     (Synthetic glm-flash declares low|high|max only) can therefore never run
     through this role, so the adapter refuses it up front with an actionable
     message (exit 2) instead of burning a ~35s :PROCESS-FAILURE and the
     card's lease. PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_IGNORE_EFFORT=1 attempts anyway.
     STRUCTURED-SUMMARY BRIDGES (2026-09-27): the brief asks for literal
     `EVIDENCE:` / `DESTINATION: <COL>` lines, but a structured-summary model
     emits a JSON object instead. The adapter bridges BOTH: it emits a
     labeled EVIDENCE line carrying the summary when none is present, and a
     `DESTINATION:` line when the summary carries a JSON-shaped
     `"destination"` and no literal one. Without the destination bridge an
     AUTO LOGIC-REVIEW PASS that meant DONE was silently advanced one column
     into HUMAN-REVIEW — resurrecting the very human gate the AUTO lane
     exists to remove (observed live on card 1f010ba9).
     VERDICT ENFORCEMENT (2026-09-27, found by running the AUTO lane end to
     end): a REVIEW column's whole contract is a VERDICT, but with the original
     free-text output contract a reviewer answered the review question and never
     rendered one — twice (card 88a92237, real model). pp-flow advanced a column
     anyway, so \"forgot to answer\" was indistinguishable from a deliberate
     escalation: an unattended AUTO card parked in HUMAN-REVIEW on a verdict
     that was never given. Three changes:
       (1) the adapter's default output contract is now a JSON-SCHEMA object
           requiring `verdict` (PASS|FAIL|ESCALATE) and `evidence`, with
           `destination` optional. The provider VALIDATES it, so a verdict-less
           answer fails closed as :INVALID-OUTPUT instead of arriving as
           un-actionable prose. The adapter translates the object back into the
           literal EVIDENCE:/DESTINATION: lines pp-flow already parses, keeping
           ONE contract at the pp-flow boundary.
           PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_CONTRACT=string restores free text. NOTE: the
           native schema subset accepts only :type/:enum/:properties/:required/
           :additional-properties and array bounds — a :description is rejected
           as :INVALID-CONTRACT, so semantic hints belong in the prompt.
       (2) a review that still renders NO verdict gets a clearly-marked card
           comment and a stderr warning; the advance stays the conservative
           one-column step, so the failure mode is now VISIBLE rather than
           silent (on a stream with a human gate that step is the correct
           review->human handoff; on AUTO it fails SAFE toward a human instead
           of closing a card on a verdict that was never given).
       (3) the driver appends a mandatory-result-fields block to every brief.
           Appending prose alone did NOT fix this (the trust boundary is the
           provider's validation, not the prompt) — but it is kept because it
           tells a compliant model how to use the structured fields.
     HONEST LIMIT (2026-09-27): a LONG, open-ended review can exhaust a smaller
     model before it submits — observed as :PROVIDER-FAILURE \"Child stopped
     without calling yield.submit.\" after 390s / 23 provider requests. That is
     treated as terminal (it is not a transient transport fault), so the card is
     left in REVIEW with a failure comment. The schema mechanism itself is
     verified working on bounded reviews (verdict=PASS, destination=DONE,
     translated and honored). Raising reliability for long reviews — a stronger
     model for review columns, or narrowing review acceptance so the reviewer
     has a bounded job — is OPEN.
     TRANSIENT-RETRY POLICY (2026-09-27): a transient provider fault must not
     fail an unattended card — a lone Synthetic HTTP 402 ("Subscription access
     is currently blocked…") cleared on the very next attempt, while direct
     curl never failed. The adapter retries ONLY what can clear by itself:
     :PROVIDER-FAILURE whose message names HTTP 402/408/409/425/429 or 5xx,
     plus a run-job crash that wrote no job result at all (the recovery-image
     hiccup from early battle tests). Everything else is TERMINAL and runs
     exactly once — :PROCESS-FAILURE (the effort mismatch is deterministic),
     :INVALID-*, :UNKNOWN-ROLE, :ARTIFACT-FAILURE, :RUNTIME-UNAVAILABLE, and a
     :PROVIDER-FAILURE naming a non-transient status (400/401/403/404/422).
     :TIMED-OUT is terminal deliberately: the job burned its whole wall clock,
     so an immediate retry is the same coin flip for the same cost. Backoff is
     exponential (10s doubling, capped 300s) with a default budget of 2 retries
     (PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_RETRIES / PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_RETRY_BASE). Retrying is
     SAFE for the card: no job was SUCCEEDED, so nothing was written back and
     the lease is still held by this invocation — the retry cannot double-work.
     FAILURE DIAGNOSTICS (2026-09-27): the :FAILURE category + message are
     extracted with two independent sed passes; the previous single-pass
     pattern returned empty on a real :PROCESS-FAILURE (pretty-printed output
     puts a newline between :CATEGORY and its keyword), so every failure
     diagnostic read `failure=unknown`.
     POSITION (2026-09-26 plan pass): with the glochid-runner section, the
     autolith adapter becomes a FALLBACK PP_FLOW_PI runner, not the
     direction of travel — glochid is the target executor residence (run
     status, on_msg progress comments, resumable sessions). This section
     stays implemented-live and supported until the glochid runner has
     survived real cards. NOTE (2026-09-27): it is nevertheless the DEFAULT
     runner for unattended `--drain` loops — the declared fallback is what
     runs headless today, which is exactly why the hardening above matters.")
   (implements "scripts/pp-flow-autolith.sh" "scripts/pp-flow.sh (PP_FLOW_PI hook)")
   (status implemented)  ;; autolith runner exercised end-to-end by the orchestration TEST card (2026-09-25)
   (verify
    (test "PP_FLOW_PI=scripts/pp-flow-autolith.sh runs a brief and prints the :RESULT transcript on stdout"
          "a SUCCEEDED job exits 0 and pp-flow scrapes EVIDENCE / DESTINATION from the transcript"
          "a failed job exits non-zero and pp-flow releases the claim without advancing"
          "a JSON destination: DONE summary yields a literal DESTINATION line (an AUTO PASS closes the card)"
          "a model rejecting the role's :reasoning-effort is refused in <1s with the accepted values named"
          "a transient HTTP 402 provider fault is retried and a later attempt SUCCEEDS (one card run)"
          "a persistent transient fault exhausts the budget and still exits non-zero"
          "a deterministic failure (:PROCESS-FAILURE, or a 401/403/404) is attempted EXACTLY once"
          "a :PROCESS-FAILURE diagnostic names the category and message, not `unknown`")))

 (section lib-hygiene
  (doc "One generation of todo code, not two. The 2026-10-01 audit found
   `libs/todo/lib.pp` carrying a LEGACY layer beside the v2 card layer:
   ~130 defines outside the 41-name `(package todo (export …))` manifest,
   including two complete UI generations and two complete data-access
   generations.

   MEASURED (live 2026-10-01):
     LIVE v2          | DEAD twin
     todo/ui/{main,detail,nav,handle-post} | todo/{main-html,detail-html,
                                               nav-html,items-html,handle-post}
     todo/item/list-li, todo/item/html     | todo/list-item-li, todo/item-li
     todo/state/options*, todo/state/*     | todo/state-options*,
                                               todo/state-options-from, todo/states
     todo/tree/* (all data access)         | todo/db/* (rows, create, update,
                                               get, insert-by-created, node-row,
                                               path-id, prefix, sort-by-created,
                                               delete, update-field) — ZERO callers
   Plus 19 alias pairs with identical bodies: todo/open? == todo/state/open?;
   todo/list == todo/list/all == todo/lists; todo/item-state == todo/item/state;
   todo/item-list-id == todo/item/list-id; todo/create-list == todo/list/create;
   todo/delete == todo/item/delete; todo/item-due == todo/item/due;
   todo/state-options-from == todo/state/options-from;
   todo/nav-link == todo/ui/nav-link; todo/ui/view == todo/view; and others.

   LATENT BUG IN THE DEAD PATH: `todo/item-li` posts `action=toggle`, but
   the live `todo/ui/handle-post` has NO toggle case — it falls through its
   `else` to `todo/item/add`, so re-wiring the legacy UI would CREATE A CARD
   on every toggle click. It is dead today and a loaded trap tomorrow.
   `todo/partial` is exported but bound to no route.

   DECISION: the legacy layer is DELETED, not deprecated-but-kept. The v2
   layer is the only one with events, comments, pipelines, and recipes —
   keeping a second data-access path is exactly how two storage models grow
   back. The `(package todo (export …))` manifest is synced to reality in
   the same pass: every live cross-package symbol is exported, deleted
   symbols leave the manifest, and a bookkeeping check
   (scripts/verify_libs_layout.sh's sibling for exports) fails loudly when
   a lib calls an unexported todo symbol.

   NAMING NOTE (why this is safe now but was not before): the dead layer
   was reachable only through `todo/main-html`/`todo/partial`, neither of
   which any route or package binds (verified by an exhaustive caller
   sweep). Deletion is therefore a pure subtraction — no product behavior
   changes, which the loop proves by diffing `/todos`, `/kanban`, and
   `/workstreams` renders before and after.")
  (implements "libs/todo/lib.pp (legacy layer removal + export manifest sync)"
              scripts/verify_todo_exports.sh)
  (status implemented)  ;; T4 lib-hygiene (2026-10-01): 41 legacy defines deleted, export manifest synced 64->88, scripts/verify_todo_exports.sh guards it
  (verify
   (test "no define named todo/db/* or todo/main-html|detail-html|nav-html|items-html|handle-post|list-item-li|item-li|partial remains"
         "no duplicate-alias pairs remain (one canonical name per body)"
         "the export manifest equals the set of todo symbols called from outside the package"
         "scripts/verify_libs_layout.sh (or its export check) fails when a lib calls an unexported todo symbol"
         "/todos, /kanban, /workstreams render byte-identical content before and after the deletion")))

 (section glochid-runner
  (doc "The TARGET executor residence: runs move from fire-and-forget
   subprocesses (PP_FLOW_PI: `pi -p`, the autolith adapter) into RESIDENT
   GLOCHID (glochid.borg — the local-first harness with sessions-persist, the
   filmstrip shell, and the habitat bridge). What this buys: the session IS
   the run record (session-is-the-run — observable, resumable, a session
   JSONL per run instead of a scraped log file), progress comments fall out
   of the loop's on_msg sink (posted to the card feed through the bridge),
   multiple runs execute as fibers in ONE process under the operator's
   watch, and the /workstreams board can show live status off the card feed.
   SHAPE: pp-flow's run mode shrinks to claim (lease = glochid:<session-id>
   @<ts>) -> fetch brief -> submit to resident glochid (localhost endpoint
   or drop-dir, the glochid-served pattern) -> poll the SESSION's terminal
   marker -> write-back from terminal status. Glochid never writes tree
   rows locally (it has no lens write path today and must not grow one for
   runs — glochid/lib surface.ml: 'a lens-backed row is the deferred
   unification'); everything habitat-side lands via bridge comments.
   Later, the dispatch loop (poll TODO across workstreams, claim,
   submit, concurrency cap, park-on-failure) moves INSIDE resident glochid
   as a plugin — the orchestrator becomes a glochid face, not a ksh service.
   HONEST STATUS (glochid.borg): agent-loop is partial (OpenAI-compat provider
   only, tool registry v0, long unattended turns unproven) — pi runs today
   because it is mature. SEQUENCING: (1) session header card key + terminal
   marker + bridge progress comments, (2)
   on_msg → progress comments, (3) pp-flow --runner glochid dogfooded on
   cheap cards alongside PP_FLOW_PI, (4) harden the loop for unattended
   runs (resume-from-JSONL, budget caps, abort), (5) flip the default and
   move dispatch inside. Do NOT flip the default until the loop has
   survived real overnight cards.")
  (implements "glochid/lib (run submission via the session seam, planned)"
              "scripts/pp-flow.sh (--runner glochid, planned)")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "a brief submitted to resident glochid opens a session with the card id in its header, streams progress comments, and ends with a terminal status marker"
         "a crashed run resumes from its session JSONL"
         "pp-flow --runner glochid completes a full claim→work→write-back card end to end")))

 (section live-face
  (doc "A `/workstreams` route (package workstreams) computed at render time:
   per-workstream column counts (open cards only), 'ready for HUMAN-REVIEW'
   rollup across streams, 'stale' (open >3d) flags, and links into
   /kanban?list=<id> + card detail. Retires the cron 'workstreams' note; the
   note is replaced by a pointer to the live face.")
  (implements libs/workstreams/lib.pp)  ;; ATOM path (not a string) so the lib binds as declared in the book manifest
  (status implemented)  ;; orchestration task 9 (2026-09-27: /workstreams live face)
  (verify
   (test "each stream shows live counts matching kanban"
         "HUMAN-REVIEW rollup lists every card in that column across streams")))

 (section standup-v2
